WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.330
Picard and his crew go undercover when they
finally reach Freecloud in the fifth episode

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:05.420
of Star Trek: Picard.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.960
From dire character fates to some surprising
nods to the past, here are all the Easter

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.700
eggs and references you missed in "Stardust
City Rag."

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.380
The fifth episode of Star Trek: Picard opens
with easily the grisliest scene in the series

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.880
so far, as an ex-Borg Starfleet officer has
his ocular implants removed.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.100
While the scene would be difficult to watch
no matter who it was on that operating table,

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:31.360
it becomes even more of a gut punch when Seven
of Nine arrives to rescue him, and reveals

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.660
that the poor guy being tortured is in fact
Icheb, who was first introduced to audiences

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.520
during Star Trek: Voyager's sixth season.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:38.960
My name was Icheb."

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.660
"Your name is Icheb."

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:43.980
"I remember now."

00:00:43.989 --> 00:00:48.321
Icheb was one of several young Borg drones
recovered by the Voyager crew in the episode

00:00:48.321 --> 00:00:53.180
"Collective," in which the ship comes across
a Borg cube manned only by five Borg children

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:54.820
who have been cut off from the Collective.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.039
Seven served as a surrogate parent to Icheb
and the other ex-Borg children, and nurtured

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:04.780
Icheb as he demonstrated an advanced aptitude
for astrophysics, and voiced a desire to eventually

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.250
enter Starfleet Academy.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.740
However, although an adult Icheb made an appearance
in the Voyager episode "Shattered," which

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:16.939
takes place in an alternate 2394, we now know
that in the prime Star Trek timeline, Icheb

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:21.420
tragically didn't make it past 2386, although
he did at some point achieve his dream of

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.320
becoming a Starfleet officer.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.500
When we finally meet Dr. Bruce Maddox in the
fifth episode of Star Trek: Picard, he's not

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.360
looking too great.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:33.620
Fearing for his life, the cyberneticist is
hiding out on Freecloud, seeking aid from

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:38.619
a crime lord named Bjayzl . At first, Bjayzl
orders Maddox killed, but then thinks twice

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:42.619
about it and decides to try to find out who
he's running from, thinking she might profit

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.260
from trading him to his pursuers.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:49.620
In her attempt to butter the traumatized doctor
up, Bjayzl offers him a drink of "tranya,"

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.540
a bright yellow beverage that she keeps in
a crystal decanter.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:57.619
Star Trek has long been characterized by its
distinctive food and drink, and tranya actually

00:01:57.619 --> 00:02:01.219
dates all the way back to the earliest episodes
of the original series.

00:02:01.219 --> 00:02:05.289
The beverage first appeared in the episode,
"The Corbomite Maneuver," in which it is offered

00:02:05.289 --> 00:02:07.760
to the crew of the Enterprise as a gesture
of goodwill.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.820
Tranya has since popped up here and there
throughout the Star Trek universe.

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:12.840
"Two tranyas please, very cold."

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.230
Unfortunately for Maddox, the drink that was
originally introduced as a way to signify

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:21.760
trust and friendship didn't carry the same
significance in Picard; Bjayzl used it to

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.260
drug the scientist, rendering him unconscious
so she could keep him as her prisoner.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:31.599
Several times throughout "Stardust City Rag,"
characters mention that ex-Borg Seven of Nine

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.920
originated in the Delta Quadrant, relaying
that information with an air of near-awe.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.500
"Seven, the ex-Borg Fenris ranger from the
Delta Quadrant."

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.220
This is, of course, a reference to the mysterious
area of the galaxy that served as the setting

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:48.380
for Star Trek: Voyager, where Seven of Nine
was first introduced.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.400
While Starfleet has a decent presence in the
other three Quadrants of the galaxy, the Delta

00:02:53.409 --> 00:02:57.830
Quadrant is full of unknowns due to how far
removed it is from the rest of the Federation;

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.560
when the Voyager first became stranded in
the Delta Quadrant, they estimated it would

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.020
take them 75 years to return to familiar space.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.380
While in the Delta Quadrant, the crew of Voyager
encountered a number of new alien species,

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.570
but the most powerful force in the Delta Quadrant
was actually one that had been introduced

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:15.880
much earlier in Star Trek lore the Borg.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:21.560
The Borg's first appearance was in The Next
Generation season 2 episode "Q Who", in which

00:03:21.569 --> 00:03:26.730
the alien Q flings the Enterprise deep into
uncharted space -- in this case, the Beta

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.390
Quadrant -- to prove to them that they're
not yet ready to handle all the challenges

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:31.380
of the universe.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.720
"You judge yourselves against the pitiful
adversaries you've encountered so far.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.480
The Romulans, the Klingons.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.700
Although the species originated in the Delta
Quadrant, it was this encounter with the Enterprise

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:46.020
that prompted the Borg to travel to Federation
space, with the goal of assimilating its many

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:46.520
planets.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.820
As soon as the La Sirena draws near to Freecloud,
its crew becomes inundated with the Star Trek

00:03:51.829 --> 00:03:56.349
version of pop-up ads, in the form of holograms
that aggressively attempt to hawk their wares

00:03:56.349 --> 00:03:58.480
until they are dismissed by their targeted
customer.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:03.459
One such ad, aimed at Rios, is for a maintenance
service called The Red Bolian, which promises

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:07.829
to be able to boost the La Sirena's power
conversion efficiency up from 91%.

00:04:07.829 --> 00:04:11.470
Rios gets rid of the ad with a flick of his
fingers, but the Bolians have a long history

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:12.620
in the world of Star Trek.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.239
First introduced in the Next Generation season
1 episode "Conspiracy," the Bolians were named

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:19.340
for that episode's director, Cliff Bole.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:24.020
In that episode, it is discovered that insect-like
parasites have infected a trio of Starfleet

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:28.940
admirals and are controlling them from within,
despite their normal outward appearances.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:32.490
In "Stardust City Rag," Picard and the crew
of the La Sirena all pretend to be people

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.220
they're not in order to infiltrate Bjayzl's
operation.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:39.800
Seven is also revealed to be secretly playing
a very different game than Picard and the

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.889
others, and at the end of the episode, Maddox
is betrayed by Agnes, a person he loved and

00:04:44.889 --> 00:04:45.380
trusted.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.780
While The Red Bolian was just a silly ad,
it could also have been a subtle nod to the

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:53.090
episode's themes of misplaced trust and deceiving
appearances.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.710
Or...maybe it was just a silly ad!

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:59.810
There's a lot to take in when Rios first beams
down to the neon-lit streets of Freecloud

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:03.990
in "Stardust City Rag," but it would be hard
to miss the prominent signage for "Mr. Mot's

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:04.800
Hair Emporium."

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:09.360
A recurring character on Star Trek: The Next
Generation, Mr. Mot was a Bolian barber who

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.820
worked aboard the Enterprise.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:15.520
Despite the fact that Bolians are characteristically
bald, Commander Riker had nothing but praise

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.280
for Mr. Mot's skills, saying,

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.000
"He's the best barber in Starfleet, what can
you do?"

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.150
Mot often took it upon himself to give Picard
and other members of the Enterprise crew unsolicited

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:29.660
advice that went far outside the bounds of
his hairdressing expertise, and on a couple

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:32.560
occasions, he actually played an instrumental
role in their missions.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.259
In the fifth season Next Generation episode
"Unification," Mot provided the hairpieces

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:41.260
that allowed Picard and Data to go undercover
as Romulans in their search for Ambassador

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:41.760
Spock.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:46.440
Of course, this seems extra significant in
Picard, given its focus on both Data and the

00:05:46.449 --> 00:05:47.449
Romulan people.

00:05:47.449 --> 00:05:51.580
Later, in the sixth season episode "Starship
Mine," Picard actually claimed to be Mot,

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.700
telling a group of mercenaries who had captured
him,

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.580
I'm the barber."

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:59.680
In "Stardust City Rag," Picard dresses up
as a different character, albeit without a

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:01.490
custom Mot hairpiece this time.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:06.250
To the left of the screen when Rios beams
down to Freecloud is a yellow sign advertising

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:10.420
"Dabo Tables," one of the many diversions
offered to visitors of the Las Vegas-like

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:10.920
city.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.720
Dabo is a Ferengi game of chance, similar
to roulette.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.580
"The rules are quite simple, actually."

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:19.880
Although games of dabo have been mentioned
on several Star Trek shows, it was most prominently

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:23.720
featured on Deep Space Nine, where the dabo
wheel was a fixture in Quark's bar.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.880
Despite the game's simplicity, it's actually
extremely difficult to win due to the high

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:29.200
prevalence of cheating in the game.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:32.560
Hmmm...more subtle hints of all the trickery
waiting in the episode?

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.720
"The first rule of dabo is, watch the wheel,
not the girl."

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:41.680
Speaking of Quark, it appears the Ferengi
barkeep has successfully franchised his establishment

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.830
in the years since he was a main cast member
on Deep Space Nine, including a location on

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:46.320
Freecloud.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:50.660
When Rios beams down to the city in "Stardust
City Rag," a large sign advertising Quark's

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:55.449
Bar can be seen to the right of the screen,
along with the slogan, "What is yours is ours."

00:06:55.449 --> 00:06:59.240
This is likely a riff on the motto of the
pleasure planet Risa, which Quark visited

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:03.560
in the Deep Space Nine season 5 episode, "Let
He Who Is Without Sin…"

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:08.120
He was an enthusiastic recipient of the traditional
Risian greeting, which he appears to have

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.759
since appropriated for himself in a new form.

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:13.280
"All that is ours is yours."

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:19.259
Quark is name-dropped again when Mr. Vup tells
Rios that his references have checked out,

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:19.760
saying,

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:24.300
"Mr. Quark of Ferenginar was especially satisfied
with your handling of his trouble with the

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:24.800
Breen."

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:30.259
When Seven of Nine confronts Bjayzl in "Stardust
City Rag," she finally gives Picard the full

00:07:30.259 --> 00:07:35.090
tale of what happened to Icheb 13 years earlier:
he was stationed as a science officer aboard

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:39.370
the USS Coleman, but was on leave from his
ship in order to help Seven attempt to maintain

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:42.190
order among the people who had been left behind
by the Federation.

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:46.280
Icheb was on a recon mission near Daimanta
when his ship responded to a distress call

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:49.889
that turned out to be an ambush orchestrated
by Bjayzl and her people.

00:07:49.889 --> 00:07:54.400
Icheb's ship, the Coleman, likely refers to
a character from the original series, Dr.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:58.020
Arthur Coleman, who appeared in the season
three episode, "Turnabout Intruder."

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:01.569
In the episode, the Enterprise responds to
a distress call, and the away team finds an

00:08:01.569 --> 00:08:04.729
injured Janice Lester being tended by Arthur
Coleman.

00:08:04.729 --> 00:08:08.250
However, the distress call turns out to be
a ruse so that Lester can switch bodies with

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.259
Kirk and take control of the Enterprise.

00:08:10.259 --> 00:08:13.970
Icheb's stationing aboard the Coleman nods
to a similar scenario playing out with him,

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:18.039
in which he responds to a fake distress call,
and is ambushed so that someone else can take

00:08:18.039 --> 00:08:19.270
control of his body.

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:23.250
Unfortunately, while in "Turnabout Intruder,"
the body swap is reversed and Coleman volunteers

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:27.320
to care for Lester, Icheb meets a much darker
fate in "Stardust City Rag."

